# Forbidden patterns for Rust files (*.rs). Applied by .githooks/pre-commit.
# scaffold-extensions: rs
# Format: <regex><TAB><description>   (one per line; # comments ignored)
# Separator is TAB - patterns can contain literal `|` for ERE alternation.
# Patterns are case-SENSITIVE, scanned against the staged blob. Copy to your
# project as `.forbidden-patterns/rust.txt`.
# Each macro pattern requires an opening delimiter ( [ { after the `!` so it
# only fires on a real invocation, not an identifier or a comment mention, and
# to stay portable across GNU grep and ugrep.
# Delimiter class is [([{] - bracket expressions take literal chars, no backslashes.

(^|[^A-Za-z_])dbg![[:space:]]*[([{]	Remove dbg!() debug macro before committing
(^|[^A-Za-z_])println![[:space:]]*[([{]	Use the project logger (log/tracing), not println!()
(^|[^A-Za-z_])print![[:space:]]*[([{]	Use the project logger (log/tracing), not print!()
(^|[^A-Za-z_])eprintln![[:space:]]*[([{]	Use the project logger (log/tracing), not eprintln!()
(^|[^A-Za-z_])eprint![[:space:]]*[([{]	Use the project logger (log/tracing), not eprint!()
(^|[^A-Za-z_])todo![[:space:]]*[([{]	Unfinished code: todo!() left in - implement or open a ticket
(^|[^A-Za-z_])unimplemented![[:space:]]*[([{]	Unfinished code: unimplemented!() left in - implement before committing
(^|[^A-Za-z_])unreachable![[:space:]]*[([{]	unreachable!() panics in prod if hit - prove it or return a Result

# --- Security: TLS bypass ---
# danger_accept_invalid_certs is reqwest's TLS off-switch - the same bypass
# backend.txt bans as verify=False and frontend.txt as rejectUnauthorized: false.
# Only the literal `(true)` argument fires, and only on a line whose first
# non-blank character is not `/` or `*` - so a `//`, `///`, `//!`, `/*` or
# ` * ` block-comment continuation that merely NAMES the call stays silent,
# indented or not. That guard is leading-comment only
# (`^[[:space:]]*[^/*[:space:]]`), deliberately NOT the php-style "no `/`
# anywhere before the match": chained builder calls routinely contain a `/`
# earlier on the line (a URL in a string argument), and the php-style guard was
# measured to drop those real call sites.
# Also silent: the method definition
# (`fn danger_accept_invalid_certs(mut self, accept: bool)`), a wrapper that
# forwards the flag, a config-driven call (`(cfg.accept_invalid_certs)`), the
# `(false)` call and a `Debug` field named after it, and the word-end guard
# after `true` keeps a `(true_flag)` argument out.
# Known and accepted: a trailing comment AFTER real code on the same line
# (`let x = 1; // danger_accept_invalid_certs(true)`) still fires, because the
# guard only inspects the start of the line.
^[[:space:]]*[^/*[:space:]].*danger_accept_invalid_(certs|hostnames)[[:space:]]*\([[:space:]]*true($|[^A-Za-z0-9_])	reqwest danger_accept_invalid_certs/hostnames(true) disables TLS validation (MITM risk) - fix the cert/CA

# Optional (opt-in) — uncomment a line to enable it:
# (^|[^A-Za-z_])panic![[:space:]]*[([{]	panic!() aborts the thread - prefer returning a Result (opt-in: noisy)
# \.unwrap[[:space:]]*\(	.unwrap() panics on None/Err - prefer ? or expect() (opt-in: very FP-heavy)
# \.expect[[:space:]]*\(	.expect() panics on None/Err - prefer ? or proper handling (opt-in: very FP-heavy)
# (^|[^A-Za-z_])(TODO|FIXME|XXX)($|[^A-Za-z0-9_])	TODO/FIXME/XXX not allowed - open a ticket instead (opt-in)
# #\[allow\(	An #[allow(...)] suppresses a lint - justify in review, not silently (opt-in: common)
# `unsafe` is correct and unavoidable in an FFI/systems crate, where an always-on
# rule would fire on every line of the code that needs it. Enable it in an
# application crate that should contain none - or better, put
# `#![forbid(unsafe_code)]` in lib.rs, which the compiler enforces and a grep cannot.
# (^|[^A-Za-z_])unsafe[[:space:]]*(\{|fn[[:space:]]|impl[[:space:]])	unsafe block/fn/impl - memory safety is yours now; justify it in review (opt-in: normal in FFI/systems crates)
# The same reasoning retires `transmute`/`from_utf8_unchecked`: they are only
# reachable inside an `unsafe` block, so an always-on rule fires on deliberate,
# reviewed code rather than on a defect. A grep also cannot see comments or
# string literals - a `// SAFETY:` note naming the call, or a build-system test
# that embeds Rust source as a string, trips it just as hard. Enable it in a
# crate that should contain no `unsafe` at all. `get_unchecked` is left out even
# here: `Pin::get_unchecked_mut()` is how every hand-written Future projects its
# pin, and `unsafe fn get_unchecked(` is a method definition, not a call.
# (^|[^A-Za-z_])(transmute|transmute_copy|from_utf8_unchecked)(::<[^(]*>)?\(	transmute/from_utf8_unchecked - UB if the invariant is wrong; prefer bytemuck or str::from_utf8 (opt-in: fires on reviewed unsafe code too)
